.TH CHECK_REGEX.PY 1 "2026" "mail_filter 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
check_regex.py \- test a corpus of text lines against a mail_filter regex file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B check_regex.py
.RB [ -s | --show-found ]
.RB [ -r | --reverse ]
.I text_file
.I regex_file
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads
.I text_file
one line at a time and tests each line against every regex in
.IR regex_file ,
a file in the same
.I /pattern/flags
per-line format used by
.B refile:
(see
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5),
.B Regex files
section). It does not run mail_filter itself and does not touch Subject
decoding, MIME, or anything else milter-specific \(em it is a plain,
fast way to try a refile list against a batch of sample lines before
wiring it into a rule.
.PP
.I text_file
may be
.B -
to read from standard input.
.PP
By default, prints every line of
.I text_file
that
.B no
regex in
.I regex_file
matched \(em i.e. spam your list would currently miss.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -s ", " --show-found
Also print lines that
.B did
match, together with the specific regex (file and line number) that
matched them. Without this, matched lines are silently counted, not
shown.
.TP
.BR -r ", " --reverse
Invert the pass/fail logic: report lines that
.B did
match as the problem cases instead of lines that didn't. Run against a
corpus of known-legitimate Subject lines, this finds false positives \(em
combine with
.B --show-found
to see which regex is too broad.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean: in the default direction, every line of
.I text_file
matched some regex; with
.BR --reverse ,
no line matched any regex.
.TP
.B 1
Not clean: in the default direction, at least one line of
.I text_file
matched nothing (a spam sample your list would currently miss); with
.BR --reverse ,
at least one line matched something it shouldn't have (a likely false
positive). Suitable for a pre-deploy check in a script.
.TP
.B 2
.I regex_file
failed to parse, contained no regexes, or
.I text_file
could not be opened.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find spam Subject lines a list doesn't catch yet:
.PP
.nf
.RS
check_regex.py known_spam_subjects.txt spam_subjects.regex
.RE
.fi
.PP
Same, but also show what already matches, and which rule:
.PP
.nf
.RS
check_regex.py known_spam_subjects.txt spam_subjects.regex --show-found
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check a list doesn't accidentally catch legitimate mail:
.PP
.nf
.RS
check_regex.py known_good_subjects.txt spam_subjects.regex --reverse
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/libexec/mail_filter/check_regex.py\ \ (FreeBSD)
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/mail_filter/check_regex.py\ \ (Linux)
Installed tool. Deliberately not on
.IR $PATH ;
run it with the full path shown above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mail_filter (8),
.BR mail_filter_rules.conf (5),
.BR generate_emoji_regex.py (1)
